and most of all the brands.

00:30:10.340 --> 00:30:11.752
- How, in what way would it benefit?

00:30:11.752 --> 00:30:14.230
- Most brands can't
make relevant creative,

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:16.500
because they're not making enough creative

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:18.662
to hit the many different cohorts

00:30:18.662 --> 00:30:20.081
they need to be talking to,

00:30:20.081 --> 00:30:24.393
so they end up making one
vanilla piece of video.

00:30:24.393 --> 00:30:25.850
- I know exactly what you mean.

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:28.243
Don't speak because I
want to just get this done

00:30:28.243 --> 00:30:29.710
in your great words.
- You got it.

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:32.010
- I'm sorry, "They're not
making enough creative

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:34.010
"to hit all the cohorts,

00:30:35.202 --> 00:30:37.963
"so just making one
vanilla piece of work."

00:30:37.963 --> 00:30:40.880
- We are too infatuated with reach,

00:30:42.578 --> 00:30:45.384
and not enough around relevance.

00:30:45.384 --> 00:30:48.051
If you don't make 70, 400, 3,000

00:30:48.941 --> 00:30:50.721
pieces of meaningful content,

00:30:50.721 --> 00:30:52.608
you could never create actual relevance.

00:30:52.608 --> 00:30:53.573
- Yeah, okay.

00:30:53.573 --> 00:30:55.756
- But, if you go walk
the streets right now,

00:30:55.756 --> 00:30:58.034
one of the worst words
in the creative community

00:30:58.034 --> 00:30:59.668
is volume and quantity.

00:30:59.668 --> 00:31:00.670
- That's very true, yeah.

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:01.675
- That needs to,

00:31:01.675 --> 00:31:03.754
we need to have a more thoughtful
conversation around it.

00:31:03.754 --> 00:31:05.151
- Yeah, they're bad words at the moment.

00:31:05.151 --> 00:31:06.068
- They are.

00:31:07.364 --> 00:31:09.531
- Funny enough, I was at a

00:31:11.897 --> 00:31:14.641
HBO session yesterday
where they were saying,

00:31:14.641 --> 00:31:16.308
"How can you fix your output

00:31:16.308 --> 00:31:19.631
without losing the secret source of HBO,

00:31:19.631 --> 00:31:23.690
volume, and how can you do
that without sacrificing-"

00:31:23.690 --> 00:31:25.973
- That, in the form of a one-hour drama

00:31:25.973 --> 00:31:27.999
is a far more interesting debate than

00:31:27.999 --> 00:31:31.169
trying to communicate a
toothpaste or a shampoo

00:31:31.169 --> 00:31:33.475
or a soda-pop to the end consumer, right?

00:31:33.475 --> 00:31:34.475
- Yeah.

00:31:34.475 --> 00:31:35.604
- If I'm trying to sell,

00:31:35.604 --> 00:31:39.794
what we know about long
form video theater,

00:31:39.794 --> 00:31:40.931
movies, television,

00:31:40.931 --> 00:31:43.685
is some shows are not meant for everybody.

00:31:43.685 --> 00:31:44.875
We have four people here,

00:31:44.875 --> 00:31:46.223
and that's okay.
- That's fine, yeah.

00:31:46.223 --> 00:31:48.073
- Meanwhile, we have this
audacious conversation

00:31:48.073 --> 00:31:50.857
that this 30 second spot is
supposed to make everybody

00:31:50.857 --> 00:31:52.693
inspired to buy.

00:31:52.693 --> 00:31:54.708
- That's very true.

00:31:54.708 --> 00:31:56.709
- HBO does do a lot of creative.

00:31:56.709 --> 00:31:57.709
- Yeah, they do.

00:31:57.709 --> 00:31:59.144
- They have many different shows.

00:31:59.144 --> 00:32:01.982
If HBO was a creative agency,

00:32:01.982 --> 00:32:02.980
they would have one show.

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:03.986
- Yeah.

00:32:03.986 --> 00:32:05.662
(laughs)

00:32:05.662 --> 00:32:06.579
Yeah, yeah.

00:32:08.386 --> 00:32:12.171
- I would say the inflection
points for me have been

00:32:12.171 --> 00:32:14.706
the hiring or acquisitions

00:32:14.706 --> 00:32:16.773
like PureWow and Ryan and Mary Kate.

00:32:16.773 --> 00:32:18.243
It's just been people.

00:32:18.243 --> 00:32:20.047
Our inflection point has just been people.

00:32:20.047 --> 00:32:23.318
It's when I've made good decisions
instead of bad decisions,

00:32:23.318 --> 00:32:28.068
of people who I can dance with
for a long period of time.

00:32:30.398 --> 00:32:32.308
It's unbelievable to me

00:32:32.308 --> 00:32:34.741
how much of the variable is casting.

00:32:34.741 --> 00:32:37.357
I think about business
more and more everyday

00:32:37.357 --> 00:32:39.595
like I think about sports, right?

00:32:39.595 --> 00:32:42.083
When the Knicks had Clyde
Frazier and Willis Reed

00:32:42.083 --> 00:32:43.532
and Earl the Pearl Monroe,

00:32:43.532 --> 00:32:45.342
they won championships.

00:32:45.342 --> 00:32:47.505
When they have the bums that
have been wearing the jerseys

00:32:47.505 --> 00:32:49.126
for the last 25 years, they don't.

00:32:49.126 --> 00:32:51.266
A lot of people talk
about teams as being good

00:32:51.266 --> 00:32:52.785
and not good.

00:32:52.785 --> 00:32:54.593
It's the casting within it.

00:32:54.593 --> 00:32:56.692
We have a lot of the casting,

00:32:56.692 --> 00:32:59.369
it's been the human decision-making.

00:32:59.369 --> 00:33:02.340
It's been the 17 to 44 people

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:04.579
that have disproportionately
impacted the business,

00:33:04.579 --> 00:33:06.188
and every time I made that right call.

00:33:06.188 --> 00:33:07.871
And by the way, every time
I made the wrong call,

00:33:07.871 --> 00:33:10.371
it's slowing down opportunity.

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:13.732
To me, it's very heavily
predicated on people.

00:33:13.732 --> 00:33:14.565
- Totally.

00:33:15.499 --> 00:33:18.166
(pensive music)

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:29.707
- Guys, the amount of you

00:33:29.707 --> 00:33:32.876
that are not subscribed to
this channel is devastating.

00:33:32.876 --> 00:33:34.955
Please, subscribe and leave a comment.

00:33:34.955 --> 00:33:37.039
Let me know how you liked
the three day mash-up.

00:33:37.039 --> 00:33:38.956
We're always listening.

